<!--
  Stream activation - when a node judges whether its periodic streams keep arriving.

  A receiver supervises each periodic stream with a reception deadline. It judges none of them
  until two gates are open: a start delay has passed since the first poll that had a clock, and a
  condition the application supplies holds (the supply is in range, the bus is up). Every time
  the two gates open together, the deadlines of every stream that can time out are counted again
  from that moment, so a period with the gates shut is never charged to a stream, and a stream
  that never arrived times out one deadline after the activation.

  Division of labour with the host (the machine owns the states, the host owns the clock and the
  condition):
    - The host polls. A poll that has a clock raises `clock.seen`; whether the start delay has
      passed is a comparison of three numbers over the time source (`tick_elapsed`), the host's,
      which raises `delay.over`. A poll without a clock judges nothing, so the host raises
      `condition.fails` for it: the next poll with a clock activates again and re-bases.
    - The condition is the application's value, and the host raises `condition.holds` or
      `condition.fails` on every poll, as the level it is. The edge is the machine's: only the
      transition into `Active` re-bases the streams.
    - The streams, their deadlines and their error counts are the host's table (one row per
      stream, a keyed table). The machine says WHEN the table is re-based and its counts reset.

  The states:
    Unclocked - no poll has had a clock yet; the start delay has not begun.
    Delaying  - the start delay runs from the first poll that had a clock.
    Inactive  - the delay has passed and the condition does not hold. Nothing is judged.
    Active    - the delay has passed and the condition holds. Every poll judges the streams.

  Engine-free: every effect is a no-arg `<sce:action>` host-trait call, so the generated
  `StreamActivationPolicy<A>` carries no `IScriptEngine` and compiles `no_std`.

  Host actions (the generated `StreamActivationActions` trait):
    begin_delay - entered Delaying: the start delay is counted from this poll's time.
    activate    - entered Active: re-base every stream that can time out at this poll's time,
                  and reset the error counts of every stream and of the node.

  Events fed by the host:
    clock.seen      - a poll had a clock.
    delay.over      - the start delay has passed.
    condition.holds - the application's condition holds at this poll.
    condition.fails - it does not, or the poll had no clock.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:schemaLocation="http://sce.dev/ext sce-forge.xsd"
       sce:kind="statechart"
       name="stream_activation"
       version="1.0"
       initial="Unclocked">

  <state id="Unclocked">
    <transition event="clock.seen" target="Delaying"/>
  </state>

  <state id="Delaying">
    <onentry>
      <sce:action name="begin_delay" effect="duty"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="delay.over" target="Inactive"/>
  </state>

  <state id="Inactive">
    <transition event="condition.holds" target="Active"/>
  </state>

  <!-- No transition on condition.holds: the condition is raised as a level on every poll, and
       only the edge into Active re-bases the streams. -->
  <state id="Active">
    <onentry>
      <sce:action name="activate" effect="duty"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="condition.fails" target="Inactive"/>
  </state>

</scxml>
